-- Workflow templates to Anthropic best practice (board task 472d5672, stage 2).
--
-- WHY: the trial of one Planner doing requirements, design and plan in one
-- conversation beat the old chain of separate agents, and the approval now sits
-- on the plan (before any code) and on the final release check. Every build
-- template becomes: Plan (Planner, frontier, approval) -> Build (standard) ->
-- Code review (frontier) -> Test and release summary (standard, approval). Spikes
-- become Research and recommend (approval) -> Record the decision. Bug Fix moves
-- its diagnosis to the Planner. Infrastructure Change drops the post-deploy check
-- (it caught nothing in 34 runs). Idea Validation is one step. Effort is not
-- stored per step; it follows the model tier.
--
-- The step lists below are generated from docs/stage2-templates.json and pinned
-- to it by src/lib/workflow-library-stage2.test.ts, which also checks the platform
-- wording never names a person, a machine or a site.
--
-- 1. Seventeen library templates get new steps and descriptions.
-- 2. Design Review, Product Launch and Client Project are switched off
--    (is_active = false; nothing deleted, so copies on boards are unaffected).
-- 3. "Planner" is added to every kit that ships one of these templates, because
--    each template now starts with a Planner step. Platform agents are cloned
--    by role when a kit is applied, so new projects get a Planner. Guarded so a
--    re-run adds nothing.
--
-- SCOPE: platform library and kits only. Board copies are updated through the
-- MCP tools after merge (never-started runs are swapped by migration 00188's
-- function). Apply 00188 before this file.

UPDATE public.workflow_library_templates
SET steps = $tpl$[{"role":"Planner","title":"Plan","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Do your Planner job for this card, all in this one conversation. Read the card, its comments and the code and data it touches, and check facts rather than planning from assumptions. Write the requirements: what changes and why, testable acceptance checks, and on its own line 'User-facing change: YES' or 'User-facing change: NO'. If YES, design the change as an HTML mock that matches the app's existing look and components, and attach it to the card; if the card already has an approved design, use it rather than redesigning. Then write the technical plan the Build step will follow: the files to change and why, any data or interface changes, the tests to write, the risks, and the release order. If the work is too big for one change, split it into stages that each ship on their own and plan stage 1 in full. Start the working-notes file for this card at docs/notes/<first 8 characters of the card id>.md: what you decided, why, what you ruled out, and where things are. The next step reads it. Begin your output with a plain-English summary for whoever approves the plan: what will change, and each decision they need to make, with your recommendation.","deliverables":["Plain-English summary and decisions","Requirements with acceptance checks and the User-facing change line","Design mock attached to the card, if user-facing","Technical plan (stage 1 in full if staged)","Working-notes file"],"requires_approval":true},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Build","model_tier":"standard","description":"Build the approved plan (stage 1 only, if it's staged), working from the Plan step's output and the working-notes file. Write failing tests first where practical. If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, don't improvise: call fail_step with what you found and reset to Plan. If there's an approved mock, it's the source of truth for everything visual: compare what you built with it at 375px and 1280px wide and fix every difference. Add what you decided and why to the working-notes file, and commit it with your work. Return the PR link, the test and type-check results, and any deviations from the plan, with reasons.","deliverables":["Code and tests","PR link","Test and type-check results","Deviations from the plan, with reasons"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Code Reviewer","title":"Code review","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Review the PR independently against the approved plan and the working-notes file. You didn't build it, and you don't edit code. Check that it does what the plan says and nothing it doesn't. Look for bugs, security issues, whether the tests would catch a regression, and the project's conventions. Send blocking issues back with fail_step as a numbered list: reset to Build for code problems, or to Plan if the plan itself was wrong. Start your output with 'Pass' or 'Blocked'; put non-blocking notes after it.","deliverables":["Review report: Pass or numbered blocking issues"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"QA Engineer","title":"Test and release summary","model_tier":"standard","description":"Test the change independently against every acceptance check in the plan and the edge cases it lists: on the live site if it has been released, otherwise on a local or preview build. If there's an approved mock, compare the built screens with it at 375px and 1280px and attach the screenshots. Send any real failure back with fail_step and reset to Build. Then write the release summary in plain English: what's live, what you checked and how, and anything still needing a person. The release is approved from this step.","deliverables":["Test report against the acceptance checks","Screenshots, if there's a mock","Plain-English release summary"],"requires_approval":true}]$tpl$::jsonb,
    description = $tpl$Plan, build, check, release. One Planner writes the requirements, any design mock and the technical plan in one conversation, and you approve it. A cheaper model builds it, then an independent code review and an independent test follow, and you approve the release. Three hand-overs instead of seven.$tpl$,
    updated_at = now()
WHERE name = $tpl$Feature Development$tpl$;

